= 1B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= a
{parent=1b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $z=x+iy$. Using the <complex conjugate>, the squared <complex modulus> on the left is $(\alpha+\beta)^2x^2+(\alpha-\beta)^2y^2$. Squaring the equality and dividing by $(\alpha-\beta)^2$, which is nonzero, gives
$$
x^2+y^2-4\sqrt{\alpha\beta}\,x-(\alpha-\beta)^2=0.
$$
Completing the square identifies the candidate <circle>:
$$
\boxed{(x-2\sqrt{\alpha\beta})^2+y^2=(\alpha+\beta)^2.}
$$
Its **centre is $2\sqrt{\alpha\beta}$ on the real axis and its radius is $\alpha+\beta$** in the <complex plane>.

It remains to exclude extraneous points introduced by squaring. On this <circle>, $x\geq 2\sqrt{\alpha\beta}-(\alpha+\beta)$, so the original right-hand side satisfies
$$
2\sqrt{\alpha\beta}\,x+(\alpha-\beta)^2\geq(\alpha+\beta)(\alpha+\beta-2\sqrt{\alpha\beta})>0.
$$
Thus taking the nonnegative square root recovers the original equality at every point of the <circle>. This is a <circle from an affine complex-modulus equation>, with the sign check essential to the geometric conclusion.

= b
{parent=1b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $q=e^{i\theta}$. Since $q\ne1$, the <finite geometric series> gives
$$
\sum_{m=1}^N q^m=\frac{q(1-q^N)}{1-q}=\frac{q-1-q^{N+1}+q^N}{2(1-\cos\theta)},
$$
where the second equality multiplies numerator and denominator by $1-\overline q$. Taking imaginary parts gives the <finite trigonometric sum>
$$
\boxed{\sum_{m=1}^N\sin(m\theta)=\frac{\sin\theta+\sin(N\theta)-\sin((N+1)\theta)}{2(1-\cos\theta)}.}
$$
Taking real parts of the same <finite geometric series> gives
$$
\boxed{\sum_{m=1}^N\cos(m\theta)=\frac{\cos\theta-1+\cos(N\theta)-\cos((N+1)\theta)}{2(1-\cos\theta)}.}
$$
The denominator is nonzero by the restriction on $\theta$. Equivalently, these <finite trigonometric sums> are $\sin(N\theta/2)\sin((N+1)\theta/2)/\sin(\theta/2)$ and $\sin(N\theta/2)\cos((N+1)\theta/2)/\sin(\theta/2)$, respectively.

= 2C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= Solution
{parent=2C}

Denote the four displayed matrices, from left to right, by $T_1,T_2,T_3,T_4$. An <orthogonal matrix> has an orthonormal set of columns, equivalently $T^TT=I$. Direct column <dot products> give $T_j^TT_j=I$ for $j=1,2,4$. The first column of $T_3$ has squared <Euclidean norm> $(1+6+1)/6=4/3$, so **the third matrix is the unique nonorthogonal matrix**.

For $T_1$, the <determinant> is $1$. A nonidentity <orthogonal transformation> of $\mathbb R^3$ with <determinant> $1$ is a rotation: it has a fixed axis and restricts to a plane rotation on its <orthogonal complement>. Consequently **$T_1$ is the rotation**.

For $T_2$, calculation gives
$$
\det T_2=-1,\qquad \det(T_2-I)=-\frac43.
$$
A plane <reflection (mathematics)> fixes its entire plane, and therefore has <eigenvalue> $1$. Since $T_2-I$ is invertible, $T_2$ is not a plane <reflection (mathematics)>. Thus **$T_2$ is the combination of a rotation and a reflection**. More explicitly, $(1,-2,0)^T$ is an <eigenvector> with <eigenvalue> $-1$. On the perpendicular plane, $T_2$ is a rotation with $\cos\phi=2/3$, because $\operatorname{tr}T_2=-1+2\cos\phi=1/3$. This gives a <three-dimensional improper orthogonal transformation>.

For $T_4$, set $n=(1,2,2)^T/3$. Then
$$
T_4=I-2nn^T.
$$
This <reflection matrix> reverses the normal $n$ and fixes every vector perpendicular to $n$. Therefore **$T_4$ is reflection in the plane $x+2y+2z=0$**. In particular, $\det T_4=-1$ and $\det(T_4-I)=0$. Finally **$T_3$ represents none of the three listed orthogonal transformations**, since each preserves the <Euclidean norm>.

= 3F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= a
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $0<x<\pi/2$, the usual unit-circle area comparison, with angles measured in radians, gives $\sin x\leq x\leq\tan x$. Hence
$$
\cos x\leq\frac{\sin x}{x}\leq1.
$$
The <cosine> is a <continuous function> at zero, so the <squeeze theorem> yields the right-hand <limit of a function> $1$. The quotient $\sin x/x$ is even, so the left-hand <limit of a function> is the same. Thus **the limit is $1$**.

= b
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $x$ sufficiently close to zero, $1+x>0$. Apply the <mean value theorem> to the <logarithm> between $1$ and $1+x$: there is a point $\xi_x$ between these endpoints with
$$
\frac{\log(1+x)}{x}=\frac1{\xi_x}.
$$
As $x\to0$ from either side, $\xi_x\to1$, so this quotient tends to $1$. The <exponential function> is continuous, and therefore
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}(1+x)^{1/x}=\lim_{x\to0}\exp\!\left(\frac{\log(1+x)}x\right)=e.}
$$
The hypotheses of the <mean value theorem> hold because $\log t$ is continuous and differentiable for $t>0$, with derivative $1/t$.

= c
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $x>0$, the exponent $x/(1+x)$ lies in $(0,1)$, and $0\leq\cos^4x\leq1$. Consequently
$$
0\leq\frac{(1+x)^{x/(1+x)}\cos^4x}{e^x}\leq\frac{1+x}{e^x}\leq\frac{2(1+x)}{x^2}.
$$
The last inequality uses the positive-term <power series> $e^x=\sum_{k\geq0}x^k/k!\geq x^2/2$ for $x>0$. The final bound tends to zero, so the <squeeze theorem> gives **limit $0$**.

= 4E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= Solution
{parent=4E}

A complex <power series> with <radius of convergence> $R$ converges absolutely at every $z$ with $|z|<R$ and diverges at every $z$ with $|z|>R$. This makes no general assertion at $|z|=R$: boundary points must be examined separately. The series always converges at $z=0$. If $R=0$, no nonzero point lies in the convergence disk; if $R=\infty$, the series converges absolutely throughout the <complex plane>.

If $p$ is the zero polynomial, every coefficient vanishes, so **$R=\infty$**. Otherwise let $d$ be its degree and $c\ne0$ its leading coefficient. Since $p(n)=cn^d(1+o(1))$,
$$
\left|\frac{p(n+1)}{p(n)}\right|\longrightarrow1.
$$
The <ratio test> therefore proves absolute convergence when $|z|<1$. For $|z|>1$, the terms $p(n)z^n$ do not tend to zero, so the series diverges. Thus the <polynomial-coefficient power series> has **$R=1$ for every nonzero polynomial**. In fact, it also diverges at every $|z|=1$, because $|p(n)z^n|=|p(n)|$ does not tend to zero, whether $d=0$ or $d>0$.

= 5B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= i
{parent=5b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> in $\mathbb R^n$ is
$$
\boxed{|a\cdot b|\leq\|a\|\,\|b\|.}
$$
Here the <Euclidean norm> is induced by the <dot product>. If $b=0$, the result is immediate. If $b\ne0$, the squared <Euclidean norm> of the component of $a$ perpendicular to $b$ is nonnegative:
$$
0\leq\left\|a-\frac{a\cdot b}{\|b\|^2}b\right\|^2=\|a\|^2-\frac{(a\cdot b)^2}{\|b\|^2}.
$$
Rearrangement proves the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>. Equality holds precisely when $a$ and $b$ are linearly dependent, including the cases of a zero vector.

Expanding the squared <Euclidean norm> and using the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives
$$
\|a+b\|^2=\|a\|^2+2a\cdot b+\|b\|^2\leq(\|a\|+\|b\|)^2.
$$
Taking nonnegative square roots gives the <triangle inequality>. Applying the <triangle inequality> once more gives
$$
\boxed{\|a+b\|\leq\|a\|+\|b\|,\qquad\|a+b+c\|\leq\|a\|+\|b\|+\|c\|.}
$$

= ii
{parent=5b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Subtract the two constraints to obtain $x\cdot(a-b)=A-B$. The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives $|A-B|\leq\|x\|\,\|a-b\|$. Since $a\ne b$, division and squaring give the <distance bound from two hyperplane constraints>
$$
\boxed{\|x\|^2\geq\frac{(A-B)^2}{\|a-b\|^2}.}
$$
If $a=b$ and $A\ne B$, **the intersection is empty**. If $a=b\ne0$ and $A=B$, the constraints describe the same hyperplane. Its points satisfy $\|x\|^2\geq A^2/\|a\|^2$, with equality at $x=Aa/\|a\|^2$. The fraction involving $a-b$ is undefined in this case and cannot be used.

If $a=b=0$, both equations are satisfied by every $x$ when $A=B=0$, and otherwise there is no solution. These degenerate cases distinguish an inconsistent constraint from a repeated one.

= iii
{parent=5b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Apply the <triangle inequality> to the path from $x_1$ through $y_2$ to $y_1$:
$$
\|x_1-y_1\|-\|x_1-y_2\|\leq\|y_1-y_2\|.
$$
This is one direction of the <reverse triangle inequality>. A second application of the <triangle inequality>, this time through $x_2$, gives
$$
\boxed{\|x_1-y_1\|-\|x_1-y_2\|\leq\|y_1-y_2\|\leq\|x_2-y_1\|+\|x_2-y_2\|.}
$$
The resulting <four-point triangle inequality> follows without any assumptions on coincidences among the four points.

= 6C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= i
{parent=6c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write a vector in the domain as $(u,v,w,t)^T$. The first and third equations defining the <kernel of a linear map> imply $v=2\alpha u-2t$. Substitution into the second equation then gives
$$
2(\alpha-1)\bigl(t-(\alpha+1)u\bigr)=0.
$$
For $\alpha\ne1$, this forces $t=(\alpha+1)u$, $v=-2u$ and $w=3u$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\ker M_\alpha=\operatorname{span}\{(1,-2,3,\alpha+1)^T\},\qquad\operatorname{im}M_\alpha=\mathbb R^3\quad(\alpha\ne1).}
$$
The <kernel of a linear map> has dimension one, so the <rank-nullity theorem> gives rank three and proves the assertion about the <image of a linear map>. Alternatively, the minor formed from columns $2,3,4$ has <determinant> $2(\alpha-1)$, including a nonzero value at $\alpha=-1$.

For $\alpha=1$, the parameters $u,t$ are free and $v=2u-2t$, $w=-3u+3t$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\ker M_1=\operatorname{span}\{(1,2,-3,0)^T,(0,-2,3,1)^T\}.}
$$
The third row is the first row minus the second. The <image of a linear map> is therefore contained in $y_1-y_2-y_3=0$. The first and third columns, $(1,2,-1)^T$ and $(1,0,1)^T$, are linearly independent, so the <matrix rank> is two and this containment is equality:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{im}M_1=\{y\in\mathbb R^3:y_1-y_2-y_3=0\}=\operatorname{span}\{(1,2,-1)^T,(1,0,1)^T\}.}
$$
This is the sole <kernel jump in a parameter-dependent linear map>; there is no further exceptional case at $\alpha=-1$.

= ii
{parent=6c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $e_1,\ldots,e_n$ be the standard <basis> and define $a_j=f(e_j)$. By linearity,
$$
f(x)=f\!\left(\sum_{j=1}^n x_je_j\right)=\sum_{j=1}^n x_jf(e_j)=a\cdot x.
$$
Thus every <linear functional> has this form. Its representing vector is unique because evaluating $a\cdot x=b\cdot x$ at each $e_j$ gives $a_j=b_j$.

For the prescribed <kernel of a linear map>, the third listed vector is the first minus the second, so it adds no independent constraint. The first two require
$$
a_1+a_2+a_3-a_4=0,\qquad2a_1-a_2-2a_4=0.
$$
Taking $a_1=s$ and $a_4=t$ gives $a_2=2s-2t$, $a_3=-3s+3t$. Consequently **all possible representing vectors** are
$$
\boxed{a=s(1,2,-3,0)^T+t(0,-2,3,1)^T,\qquad s,t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Conversely, every such vector is perpendicular to each of the three prescribed vectors, so its <linear functional> vanishes on them. This also includes the zero <linear functional>.

= 7A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= i
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <characteristic polynomials>, in the convention $\det(\lambda I-T)$, are
$$
\chi_A(\lambda)=(\lambda-1)(\lambda-2)^2,\qquad\chi_B(\lambda)=(\lambda-2)(\lambda-4)(\lambda-6).
$$
Solving the <eigenvalue equations> gives the complete <eigenspaces>
$$
\begin{aligned}
E_1(A)&=\operatorname{span}\{(1,1,1)^T\},\\
E_2(A)&=\{(x,y,z)^T:y=x+z\}=\operatorname{span}\{(1,0,-1)^T,(1,2,1)^T\},\\
E_2(B)&=\operatorname{span}\{(1,1,1)^T\},\\
E_4(B)&=\operatorname{span}\{(1,0,-1)^T\},\\
E_6(B)&=\operatorname{span}\{(1,2,1)^T\}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Every nonzero vector in a listed <eigenspace> is an <eigenvector>, and these are all the <eigenvectors>. For $A$, the repeated <eigenvalue> $2$ has a two-dimensional <eigenspace>; for $B$, all three <eigenvalues> are simple. The three displayed generating vectors form a <basis> because their column matrix
$$
S=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\1&0&2\\1&-1&1\end{pmatrix}
$$
has <determinant> $2$. Thus **both matrices are diagonalizable**, with diagonal entries $(1,2,2)$ for $A$ and $(2,4,6)$ for $B$ in this <basis>.

= ii
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Suppose $C=S\Lambda_CS^{-1}$ and $D=S\Lambda_DS^{-1}$ for an invertible $S$ and diagonal matrices $\Lambda_C,\Lambda_D$. Since diagonal matrices commute,
$$
CD=S\Lambda_C\Lambda_DS^{-1}=S\Lambda_D\Lambda_CS^{-1}=DC.
$$
Therefore **simultaneous diagonalization implies commutation**. This argument works over either $\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$ and explains why <simultaneous diagonalization> produces <commuting operators>.

= iii
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $Dx=\lambda x$ with $x\ne0$. Commutation gives
$$
D(Cx)=C(Dx)=\lambda Cx.
$$
Thus $Cx$ belongs to the <eigenspace> $E_\lambda(D)$, with the zero vector allowed. Since $D$ has $n$ distinct <eigenvalues>, each <eigenspace> is one-dimensional. Hence **$Cx=\mu x$ for some scalar $\mu$**. This is the fact that <commuting maps preserve eigenspaces>.

The standard <linear independence> theorem for <eigenvectors> belonging to distinct <eigenvalues> supplies a <basis> $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ of <eigenvectors> of $D$. The preceding argument makes every one an <eigenvector> of $C$ as well. Their column matrix therefore simultaneously diagonalizes $C$ and $D$. This is <simple spectrum gives simultaneous diagonalization>.

For real matrices, this conclusion always holds over $\mathbb C$; a real common <basis> follows when the distinct <eigenvalues> of $D$ are real. Real matrix entries alone do not ensure a real <eigenbasis>: a quarter-turn of $\mathbb R^2$, with $C=D$, has <eigenvalues> $\pm i$. The matrices in this question have real spectra, so their common <basis> is real.

= iv
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Direct multiplication gives
$$
AB=BA=\begin{pmatrix}0&10&-8\\-10&22&-10\\-8&10&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Take $D=B$ and $C=A$ in the preceding result: $B$ has the three distinct <eigenvalues> $2,4,6$ and the matrices commute. The common <eigenbasis> from part (i) gives
$$
\boxed{S=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\1&0&2\\1&-1&1\end{pmatrix},\quad S^{-1}AS=\operatorname{diag}(1,2,2),\quad S^{-1}BS=\operatorname{diag}(2,4,6).}
$$
Indeed $AS=S\operatorname{diag}(1,2,2)$ and $BS=S\operatorname{diag}(2,4,6)$, and $\det S=2\ne0$. These equalities directly verify the <simultaneous diagonalization>.

= 8A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= a
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the standard complex <inner product>, $\|y\|^2=y^\dagger y$. Using the <conjugate transpose> and the defining equality for a <normal matrix>,
$$
\|Ax\|^2=x^\dagger A^\dagger Ax=x^\dagger AA^\dagger x=\|A^\dagger x\|^2.
$$
Both <norms> are nonnegative, so **$\|Ax\|=\|A^\dagger x\|$** for every $x$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $M=A-\lambda I$. Its <conjugate transpose> is $M^\dagger=A^\dagger-\overline\lambda I$, so
$$
\begin{aligned}
M^\dagger M&=A^\dagger A-\lambda A^\dagger-\overline\lambda A+|\lambda|^2I,\\
MM^\dagger&=AA^\dagger-\overline\lambda A-\lambda A^\dagger+|\lambda|^2I.
\end{aligned}
$$
Since $A$ is a <normal matrix>, the two products coincide. Therefore **$A-\lambda I$ is normal for every complex $\lambda$**.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $Ax=\lambda x$, then $M=A-\lambda I$ is a <normal matrix> by part (ii), and $Mx=0$. Part (i), applied to $M$, yields $\|M^\dagger x\|=\|Mx\|=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{A^\dagger x=\overline\lambda x.}
$$
The nonzero vector $x$ is consequently an <eigenvector> of the <conjugate transpose> with <eigenvalue> $\overline\lambda$. This <adjoint eigenvector identity for a normal matrix> will also determine the special spectral restrictions below.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <adjoint eigenvector identity for a normal matrix> gives $A^\dagger x_\lambda=\overline\lambda x_\lambda$. Hence
$$
\mu x_\lambda^\dagger x_\mu=x_\lambda^\dagger Ax_\mu=(A^\dagger x_\lambda)^\dagger x_\mu=\lambda x_\lambda^\dagger x_\mu.
$$
As $\mu\ne\lambda$, **$x_\lambda^\dagger x_\mu=0$**. Thus <eigenvectors> in distinct <eigenspaces> of a <normal matrix> are orthogonal in the complex <inner product>.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Start with the assumed <basis> of <eigenvectors>, grouping them by <eigenvalue>. In each <eigenspace>, apply the <Gram-Schmidt process>, which replaces a linearly independent list by an orthonormal list with the same span. Every new vector stays within that <eigenspace>, since an <eigenspace> is a vector subspace.

By part (iv), different <eigenspaces> are orthogonal. The combined lists therefore form an <orthonormal basis> of the entire space, still consisting of <eigenvectors>. Let $U$ have these vectors as columns. Then $U$ is a <unitary matrix>, so
$$
\boxed{U^{-1}=U^\dagger,\qquad U^\dagger AU=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n).}
$$
This gives the required <unitary diagonalization of a normal matrix>, including repeated <eigenvalues>.

= b
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <Hermitian matrix>, $A^\dagger=A$, so $A^\dagger A=A^2=AA^\dagger$ and $A$ is a <normal matrix>. If $Ax=\lambda x$ with $x\ne0$, part (a)(iii) gives $A^\dagger x=\overline\lambda x$. Since also $A^\dagger x=Ax=\lambda x$, we obtain $\lambda=\overline\lambda$. Thus **every eigenvalue is real**.

= c
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a <skew-Hermitian matrix>, $A^\dagger=-A$, so both $A^\dagger A$ and $AA^\dagger$ equal $-A^2$. It is therefore a <normal matrix>. On an <eigenvector> with <eigenvalue> $\lambda$, part (a)(iii) gives
$$
\overline\lambda x=A^\dagger x=-Ax=-\lambda x.
$$
Thus $\overline\lambda=-\lambda$, and **every eigenvalue lies in $i\mathbb R$**, including the possible <eigenvalue> zero.

= d
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For a <unitary matrix>, $A^\dagger=A^{-1}$, so both defining products for a <normal matrix> are $I$. The matrix is invertible, hence no <eigenvalue> is zero. If $Ax=\lambda x$, then $A^{-1}x=\lambda^{-1}x$, while part (a)(iii) gives $A^\dagger x=\overline\lambda x$. Therefore $\overline\lambda=\lambda^{-1}$, and
$$
\boxed{|\lambda|=1.}
$$
Thus **all eigenvalues have unit modulus**.

= 9F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= Solution
{parent=9F}

Since $a_j=S_j-S_{j-1}$, shifting an index yields the <summation by parts> identity
$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{j=m}^n a_jb_j&=\sum_{j=m}^nS_jb_j-\sum_{j=m}^nS_{j-1}b_j\\
&=S_nb_n-S_{m-1}b_m+\sum_{j=m}^{n-1}S_j(b_j-b_{j+1}).
\end{aligned}
$$
The sum on the last line is empty when $m=n$, so the identity also covers that case.

**The series with a bounded monotone multiplier converges.** Here is a proof that also permits conditional convergence. Choose $M>0$ with $|b_j|\leq M$. The <Cauchy sequence> criterion for the convergent series $\sum a_j$ ensures that, for any $\eta>0$ and sufficiently large $m$, all the partial tails $T_j=\sum_{k=m}^j a_k$ satisfy $|T_j|<\eta$. Apply <summation by parts> to these partial tails:
$$
\sum_{j=m}^n a_jb_j=T_nb_n+\sum_{j=m}^{n-1}T_j(b_j-b_{j+1}).
$$
Since the multiplier is a <monotone sequence>, $\sum_{j=m}^{n-1}|b_j-b_{j+1}|=|b_m-b_n|$. Consequently
$$
\left|\sum_{j=m}^n a_jb_j\right|\leq\eta\bigl(|b_n|+|b_m-b_n|\bigr)\leq3M\eta.
$$
Taking $\eta=\varepsilon/(3M)$ proves that the weighted partial sums form a <Cauchy sequence>, and hence converge. This is a special case of <bounded-variation multipliers of a convergent series>.

**The series $\sum n^{1/n}a_n$ also converges.** The multiplier need only be monotone eventually, since a finite initial segment cannot affect convergence. For $h(x)=\log x/x$,
$$
h'(x)=\frac{1-\log x}{x^2}<0\qquad(x\geq3).
$$
Thus $n^{1/n}=\exp(h(n))$ decreases for $n\geq3$; it is bounded and tends to $1$, because $\log n/n\to0$. Apply the proved multiplier result to the tail beginning at $3$. The fact that the first few values are not monotone causes no difficulty.

= 10D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= a
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

We use the <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem>: every bounded real sequence has a convergent subsequence. If $f$ were unbounded on $[a,b]$, there would be $x_n\in[a,b]$ with $|f(x_n)|\geq n$. The <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> gives a subsequence $x_{n_k}\to c$, and the closed interval contains $c$. Since $f$ is a <continuous function>, $f(x_{n_k})\to f(c)$, contradicting $|f(x_{n_k})|\geq n_k\to\infty$. Thus **$f$ is bounded**.

Let $s=\sup_{x\in[a,b]}f(x)$. By the definition of the <supremum>, choose $y_n$ with $s-1/n<f(y_n)\leq s$. Again the <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> gives $y_{n_k}\to d\in[a,b]$. Continuity yields $f(d)=s$. Applying the same argument to $-f$ gives a point where $f$ attains its <infimum>. Therefore **both the supremum and the infimum are attained**. This proves the <extreme value theorem> for a closed bounded interval.

= b
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Away from integer multiples of $\pi$, the function is a composition of <continuous functions>. At zero, the bound $|g(x)|\leq\sqrt{|x|}$ gives $g(x)\to0=g(0)$ by the <squeeze theorem>. This is <oscillation multiplied by a vanishing amplitude>.

Fix $n\ne0$ and set $t_k=\pi/2+2\pi k$ and $x_k=n\pi+(-1)^n\arcsin(1/t_k)$. Then $x_k\to n\pi$, $\sin x_k=1/t_k$, and
$$
g(x_k)=\sqrt{|x_k|}\longrightarrow\sqrt{|n\pi|}\ne0=g(n\pi).
$$
So $g$ is discontinuous at every nonzero integer multiple of $\pi$. Its **set of continuity points** is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb R\setminus\{n\pi:n\in\mathbb Z,\ n\ne0\}.}
$$

On $[0,\pi]$, $g(x)\leq\sqrt{x}<\sqrt\pi$ for $x<\pi$, while $g(\pi)=0$. With $x_k=\pi-\arcsin(1/t_k)$ as above, $g(x_k)=\sqrt{x_k}\to\sqrt\pi$. Consequently **the supremum is $\sqrt\pi$ and is not attained**. This illustrates that <supremum can fail to be attained at an oscillatory endpoint>.

On $[\pi,3\pi/2]$, set $x_0=\pi+\arcsin(2/(3\pi))$. It lies strictly inside the interval, and $1/\sin x_0=-3\pi/2$, so $g(x_0)=\sqrt{x_0}>\sqrt\pi$. Choose $0<\delta<x_0-\pi$. On $[\pi,\pi+\delta]$,
$$
g(x)\leq\sqrt{\pi+\delta}<\sqrt{x_0}=g(x_0).
$$
On $[\pi+\delta,3\pi/2]$, $g$ is a <continuous function>, so the <extreme value theorem> gives a maximum there, at least $g(x_0)$. It exceeds every value on the discarded interval. Therefore **$g$ does attain its supremum on $[\pi,3\pi/2]$**. This is the <interior-value criterion for an attained maximum>.

\Image[paper-1-oscillatory-suprema.png]
{height=430}
{title=Oscillatory function near pi: an unattained left supremum and a right interior value above the endpoint envelope}

= 11D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= i
{parent=11d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <intermediate value theorem> says that if $f:[a,b]\to\mathbb R$ is a <continuous function>, then every value between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ is attained at some point of $[a,b]$.

Endpoint values are already attained. Suppose $f(a)<y<f(b)$ and define $E=\{x\in[a,b]:f(x)<y\}$. It is nonempty and bounded above, so $c=\sup E$ exists. Continuity at $a$ ensures that $E$ contains points to the right of $a$, and continuity at $b$ ensures that no point sufficiently close to $b$ belongs to $E$. Hence $a<c<b$.

By the definition of the <supremum>, there are points of $E$ approaching $c$. If $f(c)>y$, continuity would exclude all such nearby points from $E$, a contradiction. If $f(c)<y$, continuity would put some point to the right of $c$ in $E$, again a contradiction. Therefore **$f(c)=y$**. When $f(a)>f(b)$, apply this argument to $-f$. This proves the <intermediate value theorem> in all cases.

= ii
{parent=11d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a chord of length $1/2$, consider the <continuous function> $h(x)=f(x+1/2)-f(x)$ on $[0,1/2]$. Since $f(0)=f(1)$,
$$
h(1/2)=f(1)-f(1/2)=-h(0).
$$
An endpoint zero already supplies the chord. Otherwise the endpoint values have opposite signs, so the <intermediate value theorem> supplies $h(\alpha)=0$ at some $\alpha\in(0,1/2)$. Thus **a horizontal chord of length $1/2$ exists**.

For each integer $n>1$, define $h_n(x)=f(x+1/n)-f(x)$ on $[0,1-1/n]$. The sampled differences telescope:
$$
\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}h_n(j/n)=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\bigl(f((j+1)/n)-f(j/n)\bigr)=f(1)-f(0)=0.
$$
If any sampled value is zero, it gives the required chord. Otherwise there must be both a positive and a negative sampled value. The <intermediate value theorem>, applied between their sample points, then gives $h_n(\alpha)=0$. Hence **a horizontal chord of length $1/n$ exists for every positive integer $n$**; for $n=1$, the endpoint chord itself suffices. This proves the <horizontal chord of reciprocal-integer length> result, without restricting $n$ to powers of two.

= 12E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= Solution
{parent=12E}

For a partition $P$, write $U(f,P)$ and $L(f,P)$ for the <upper Darboux sum> and <lower Darboux sum>. The <Riemann integrability criterion> for bounded $f$ is that for every $\varepsilon>0$ some partition satisfies $U(f,P)-L(f,P)<\varepsilon$. Thus if the gaps for $D_n$ tend to zero, the <Riemann integrability criterion> immediately proves that $f$ is <Riemann integrable>.

Conversely, suppose $f$ is <Riemann integrable>, and choose a fixed partition $P$ with $U(f,P)-L(f,P)<\varepsilon/2$. Let $r$ be the number of its interior division points and choose $M\geq1$ with $|f|\leq M$. Call a cell of $D_n$ bad when its interior contains a division point of $P$. There are at most $r$ bad cells, and their total length is at most $r/n$.

Every other cell lies in a single cell of $P$, so its oscillation is no larger than that of the containing cell. Summing the contributions of these good cells gives at most $U(f,P)-L(f,P)$, while each bad cell has oscillation at most $2M$. Therefore the <finite bad-cell estimate for Darboux sums> gives
$$
0\leq U(f,D_n)-L(f,D_n)\leq U(f,P)-L(f,P)+\frac{2Mr}{n}.
$$
For large enough $n$, the right-hand side is less than $\varepsilon$. This proves the <uniform-mesh Darboux criterion>
$$
\boxed{f\text{ is Riemann integrable}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad U(f,D_n)-L(f,D_n)\longrightarrow0.}
$$
This argument does not assume that the partitions $D_n$ are nested; in general they are not.

For the composition, $f$ takes values in $[-M,M]$. Since $g$ is continuously differentiable, the <extreme value theorem> bounds both $|g|$ and $|g'|$ on that interval. In particular, let $K=\sup_{[-M,M]}|g'|<\infty$. The <mean value theorem> states that a function continuous on the interval between $s,t$ and differentiable in its interior satisfies $g(s)-g(t)=g'(\xi)(s-t)$ for some intermediate $\xi$. Hence
$$
|g(s)-g(t)|\leq K|s-t|\qquad(s,t\in[-M,M]).
$$
On each partition cell, this bounds the oscillation of $g\circ f$ by $K$ times the oscillation of $f$, whether or not the local extrema are attained. It follows that
$$
U(g\circ f,D_n)-L(g\circ f,D_n)\leq K\bigl(U(f,D_n)-L(f,D_n)\bigr)\longrightarrow0.
$$
The composition is bounded, so the proved <uniform-mesh Darboux criterion> applies. Thus **$g\circ f$ is Riemann integrable**. The reusable fact is <Lipschitz composition preserves Riemann integrability>; continuous differentiability supplies the required bound on the range of $f$.
